This `search` fetch also lacks a `.catch()`. On rejection the error is swallowed silently, and the pagination state written optimistically just above (`setSearchTagFiltersPagination(false, '', query)`) is never reconciled, leaving the UI in an inconsistent state with no logged error.

Handle and log the rejection:

```tsx
openSearchTagFiltersPage({searchQuery: query, cursor: '', limit: CONST.SEARCH.TAG_FILTER_PAGE_SIZE})
    .then(({hasMore: newHasMore, nextCursor: newCursor}) => {
        setSearchTagFiltersPagination(newHasMore, newCursor, query);
    })
    .catch((error) => Log.warn('Failed to search tag filters', {error}))
    .finally(() => setIsLoading(false));
```

This data-fetching call chains `.then().finally()` with no `.catch()`. If `openSearchTagFiltersPage` (which calls `makeRequestWithSideEffects`) rejects, the rejection is unhandled: no error is logged and the failure is silent to both the user and developer. `.finally()` resets the loading flag but does not handle the error.

Add a `.catch()` that logs the failure so the error is debuggable:

```tsx
openSearchTagFiltersPage({searchQuery: currentQuery, cursor: currentCursor, limit: CONST.SEARCH.TAG_FILTER_PAGE_SIZE})
    .then(({hasMore: newHasMore, nextCursor: newCursor}) => {
        setSearchTagFiltersPagination(newHasMore, newCursor, currentQuery);
    })
    .catch((error) => Log.warn('Failed to load more tag filters', {error}))
    .finally(() => setIsLoading(false));
```

`parseExpenseFilters` returns `makeRequestWithSideEffects(...).then(...)` with no `.catch()`. A rejected request propagates an unhandled rejection to every caller and forces each one to handle failure separately. Handle the rejection here so the action always resolves to a well-typed result:

```tsx
return makeRequestWithSideEffects(SIDE_EFFECT_REQUEST_COMMANDS.PARSE_EXPENSE_FILTERS, {nlQuery, policyID, today})
    .then((response) => {
        if (response?.success === true && response.searchURL) {
            return {success: true, searchURL: response.searchURL, humanReadableSummary: response.humanReadableSummary ?? ''} as const;
        }
        return {success: false, message: response?.message ?? ''} as const;
    })
    .catch(() => ({success: false, message: ''} as const));
```

`handleSubmit` sets `isLoading(true)` and then calls `parseExpenseFilters(...).then(...)` with no `.catch()`. If the underlying request rejects (network failure, backend error), the `.then` callback never runs, so `isLoading` is never reset and no error message is shown — the button stays stuck in a loading state with no feedback. The PR description itself notes the button should "return an error when offline".

Add a `.catch` that resets loading and surfaces an error:

```tsx
parseExpenseFilters(trimmedQuery, policyID)
    .then((result) => {
        setIsLoading(false);
        if (!result) {
            return;
        }
        if (result.success) {
            const searchQuery = new URL(result.searchURL).searchParams.get('q') ?? '';
            onSuccess(ROUTES.SEARCH_ROOT.getRoute({query: searchQuery}));
        } else {
            setErrorMessage(result.message);
        }
    })
    .catch(() => {
        setIsLoading(false);
        setErrorMessage(translate('common.genericErrorMessage'));
    });
```

This change replaces the previous `catch (error) { Log.warn('[useAutoCreateSubmitWorkspace] Error completing onboarding', {error}); }` with an empty `catch {}`. `completeOnboarding` is a critical data-mutation (it produces the `#admins` Concierge welcome message and completes onboarding). The inline comment justifies *not blocking* navigation, but it does not justify dropping all developer visibility: a failure of this mutation now leaves no trace in the logs, making regressions hard to debug.

Keep the non-blocking behavior but preserve logging so failures remain diagnosable:

```ts
} catch (error) {
    // Swallow onboarding completion failures so a network error doesn't block workspace
    // creation or the follow-up navigation; the optimistic Onyx data is already applied.
    Log.warn('[useAutoCreateSubmitWorkspace] Error completing onboarding', {error});
}
```

(Note: this requires re-adding the `import Log from '@libs/Log';` that was removed in this PR.)

`getFileSize(file.content)` resolves from `RNFS.stat`, which rejects when the file is missing or the path is malformed (exactly the `image/*`/HEIC edge cases this PR targets). The promise chain has no `.catch()`, so on rejection the `.then()` never runs, `validateFiles()` is never called, `VALIDATED_FILE_OBJECT` is never written, and the Share button silently becomes a no-op again — the same silent failure this PR is fixing. This is a critical data-mutation path with no error handling and no feedback to the user or logs. (An earlier commit in this PR added a `.catch()` here and it was reverted.)

Add a `.catch()` that logs and still proceeds to validation (so a genuine error surfaces via `ErrorModal` instead of failing silently):

```tsx
getFileSize(file.content)
    .catch((error: unknown) => {
        Log.warn('[ShareRootPage] Failed to get file size for validation', {error});
        return undefined;
    })
    .then((size) => {
        validateFiles(
            [{name: file.id, uri: file.content, type: file.mimeType, size}],
            undefined,
            {isValidatingReceipts: false},
        );
    });
```

The `getContactPermission().then(...)` promise chain (and `requestContactPermission().then(...)` in `handleGrantPermission`) has no `.catch` handler. If the underlying permission API rejects, the failure is silent and `onFocusTextInput()` is never called, leaving the text input unfocused with no feedback.

Add a `.catch` that logs the error and still restores focus, e.g.:

```ts
getContactPermission()
    .then(async (status) => {
        // ...
    })
    .catch((error) => {
        Log.warn('Failed to read contact permission', error);
        onFocusTextInput();
    });
```

The `.catch()` handler silently swallows errors from the Plaid import promise without logging. If `importPlaidAccounts` or `refreshPolicyCompanyCardsPage` fails, the error is discarded with no diagnostic information.

Log the error before cleaning up the pending state:
```tsx
importPlaidAccountsPromise.then(refreshPolicyCompanyCardsPage).catch((error) => {
    Log.warn('[BankConnection] Plaid import failed', {error});
    pendingPlaidImports.delete(plaidImportKey);
});
```

The `.catch()` handler silently swallows errors from the Plaid import promise without logging. If `importPlaidAccounts` or `refreshPolicyCompanyCardsPage` fails, the error is discarded with no diagnostic information.

Log the error before cleaning up the pending state:
```tsx
importPlaidAccountsPromise.then(refreshPolicyCompanyCardsPage).catch((error) => {
    Log.warn('[BankConnection] Plaid import failed', {error});
    pendingPlaidImports.delete(plaidImportKey);
});
```

The `catch` block silently swallows the error without logging it. Even though the comment explains the intent (fail closed to avoid crashing the report), the error should be logged so developers can diagnose malformed chart HTML in production. Silent failures make debugging significantly harder.

Add a `Log.warn` or `Log.error` call inside the catch block:

```tsx
import Log from '@libs/Log';
// ...
} catch (error) {
    // Malformed chart HTML can make a parser throw. Fail closed (render nothing) instead of crashing the whole report.
    Log.warn('[VictoryChartProvider] Failed to process chart tree from malformed HTML', {error});
    return null;
}
```

The `.catch(() => {})` silently swallows any error from `isScreenReaderEnabled()`. Even though this is a best-effort cache warm-up, silent suppression hides potential issues. At minimum, log the error so failures are visible during debugging.

Suggested fix:
```ts
isScreenReaderEnabled()
    .then((enabled) => {
        cachedScreenReaderValue = enabled;
    })
    .catch((error) => {
        Log.warn('[Accessibility] Failed to warm screen reader cache', {error});
    });
```

`calculatePopoverPosition(anchorRef, ANCHOR_ALIGNMENT).then(...)` has no `.catch()` handler. If the promise rejects (e.g., the ref is unmounted or measurement fails), this produces an unhandled promise rejection.

Add a `.catch()` or use `async/await` with a try/catch:
```tsx
calculatePopoverPosition(anchorRef, ANCHOR_ALIGNMENT).then((pos) => {
    setAnchorPosition({
        horizontal: pos.horizontal,
        vertical: pos.vertical,
    });
    setIsVisible(true);
}).catch(() => {
    // Measurement failed (e.g., anchor unmounted) - silently skip opening
});
```

The `.then()` promise chain has no `.catch()` or error handler. If `Onyx.merge` rejects, the error will be silently swallowed as an unhandled promise rejection, and the native app close operation will never execute.

Add error handling to the promise chain:
```ts
closingPromise.then(() => {
    Navigation.clearPreloadedRoutes();
    // eslint-disable-next-line no-restricted-properties
    HybridAppModule.closeReactNativeApp({shouldSetNVP});
}).catch((error) => {
    Log.warn('Failed to merge HYBRID_APP closing state', {error});
    // Still attempt to close the app to avoid leaving the user stuck
    Navigation.clearPreloadedRoutes();
    HybridAppModule.closeReactNativeApp({shouldSetNVP});
});
```

The `await moveReceiptToDurableStorage(...)` call has no error handling. If the durable storage move fails (e.g., disk full, permission error, missing file), the error will propagate as an unhandled rejection from the now-async `addActions`, silently preventing the message from being sent with no user feedback.

Wrap the call in a try/catch and fall back to the original file URI on failure:

```ts
let resolvedFile = file;
if (file?.uri) {
    try {
        const durableUri = await moveReceiptToDurableStorage(file.uri, file.name ?? 'chat_attachment');
        resolvedFile = {...file, uri: durableUri, source: durableUri};
    } catch (error) {
        Log.warn('[addActions] Failed to move receipt to durable storage, using original URI', {error});
        resolvedFile = file;
    }
}
```

`contactImport()` returns a Promise that is handled with `.then()` but has no `.catch()` or error handling. If the device contact import fails (e.g., due to permissions or a native module error), the error will be silently swallowed.

Add a `.catch()` handler to log the error:
```ts
contactImport().then(({contactList, permissionStatus}: ContactImportResult) => {
    setContactPermissionState(permissionStatus);
    const usersFromContact = getContacts(contactList, localeCompare, formatPhoneNumber, countryCode, loginList);
    setContacts(usersFromContact);
}).catch((error) => {
    Log.warn('[useContactImport] Failed to import contacts', {error});
});
```

`prefetchOnAppStart(url, init)` is called without any error handling. If this function throws (synchronously or asynchronously), it will crash the HTTP request flow. Note that the Android native side (`MainApplication.kt`) wraps the equivalent call in `try { ... } catch (_: Throwable) {}`, suggesting errors are expected.

Wrap the call in a try/catch:
```ts
if (shouldPrefetch) {
    try {
        prefetchOnAppStart(url, init);
    } catch (error) {
        Log.warn('[HttpUtils] prefetchOnAppStart failed', error);
    }
}
```

The `.catch(() => tryMediaStore(appendTimeToFileName(newFileName)))` silently swallows the error from the first `copyToMediaStore` attempt without logging it. While retrying with a timestamped filename is a valid strategy, discarding the original error makes it difficult to debug why the first attempt failed.

Suggested fix: Log the error before retrying:
```ts
tryMediaStore(newFileName)
    .catch((error) => {
        Log.info(`[localFileDownload] copyToMediaStore failed for "${newFileName}", retrying with timestamp`, false, {error});
        return tryMediaStore(appendTimeToFileName(newFileName));
    })
```

The `.then()` chain on `moveReceiptToDurableStorage` has no `.catch()` handler. Even though `moveReceiptToDurableStorage` internally catches errors and falls back to the source URI, the promise chain itself could still reject due to unexpected errors (e.g., in the `.then()` callback). An unhandled rejection would silently prevent `navigateBack()` from being called, leaving the user stuck on the screen.

The camera capture path in this same file (lines 197 and 204) consistently includes `.catch()` handlers for its promise chains, so this gallery path should follow the same pattern for consistency.

Add a `.catch()` handler to log the error and ensure the user is not stuck:

```tsx
moveReceiptToDurableStorage(sourceUri, filename)
    .then((durableUri) => {
        setMoneyRequestOdometerImage(
            transaction,
            imageType,
            {
                uri: durableUri,
                name: filename,
                type: file.type ?? getMimeTypeFromUri(durableUri) ?? 'image/jpeg',
                size: file.size,
            },
            isTransactionDraft,
            false,
        );
        navigateBack();
    })
    .catch((error: unknown) => {
        Log.warn('Failed to move odometer receipt to durable storage', error);
        navigateBack();
    });
```

The `failureValue` does not include an `errors` field, so if the API call fails, the user receives no error feedback — the toggle silently reverts. Compare with `updateSettlementAccount` in the same file (line ~708), which sets `errors: ErrorUtils.getMicroSecondOnyxErrorWithTranslationKey('common.genericErrorMessage')` in its failure data.

Add an error message to `failureValue`:
```ts
const failureValue = {
    [programKey]: {
        shouldApplyCashbackToBill: currentValue ?? true,
        pendingFields: {shouldApplyCashbackToBill: null},
    },
    errors: ErrorUtils.getMicroSecondOnyxErrorWithTranslationKey('common.genericErrorMessage'),
};
```

The `catch` block in `handleSaveForLater` silently swallows the error without logging it. While the comment explains the intent to close regardless, the error should still be logged for debuggability. Silent failures make it difficult to diagnose issues in production.

Add a `Log.warn` call to capture the error while preserving the current flow behavior:

```ts
} catch (error) {
    // If draft save fails, we still close to match "save for later" flow behavior.
    Log.warn('Failed to save odometer draft', {error});
}
```

This is consistent with the error handling pattern already used in `serializeOdometerDraftImage` and `deserializeOdometerDraftImage` in `OdometerTransactionUtils.ts`.

The optimistic data now explicitly overrides `originalAmount` and `originalCurrency` (setting them to `null` when they match `amount`/`currency`), but the `failureData` block at lines 1081-1090 does not restore these fields. If the API call fails, `originalAmount` and `originalCurrency` will remain `null` instead of being reverted to their original values from `transaction.originalAmount` and `transaction.originalCurrency`.

Every field explicitly overridden in `optimisticData` should have a corresponding restoration in `failureData`. Add the original values to the failure block:

```ts
failureData.push({
    onyxMethod: Onyx.METHOD.MERGE,
    key: `${ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.TRANSACTION}${transaction.transactionID}`,
    value: {
        reportID: transaction.reportID,
        comment: transaction.comment,
        originalAmount: transaction.originalAmount,
        originalCurrency: transaction.originalCurrency,
        ...(shouldClearAmount && {pendingAction: transaction.pendingAction ?? null}),
        ...(shouldClearAmount && {convertedAmount: transaction.convertedAmount}),
    },
});
```

The function signature was widened to accept `currencyCode: string | undefined`, but no fallback to a default currency (e.g., `CONST.CURRENCY.USD`) was added. When `currencyCode` is `undefined`, it is passed directly as `currency: undefined` to `Intl.NumberFormat` (via `format()`), which will throw a `RangeError: Invalid currency code : undefined`.

The original `convertToDisplayString` in `src/libs/CurrencyUtils.ts` (line 101) has both a default parameter (`currency: string = CONST.CURRENCY.USD`) and an explicit runtime fallback (`if (!currency) { currencyWithFallback = CONST.CURRENCY.USD; }`). The hook version has neither, breaking behavior parity.

Add a fallback for `currencyCode` before passing it to `format()`, matching the original function's behavior:

```tsx
const convertToDisplayString = useCallback(
    (amountInCents: number | undefined, currencyCode: string | undefined): string => {
        const currencyWithFallback = currencyCode || CONST.CURRENCY.USD;
        const decimals = getCurrencyDecimals(currencyWithFallback);
        const convertedAmount = convertToFrontendAmountAsInteger(amountInCents ?? 0, decimals);
        return format(preferredLocale, convertedAmount, {
            style: 'currency',
            currency: currencyWithFallback,
            minimumFractionDigits: decimals,
            maximumFractionDigits: 2,
        });
    },
    [getCurrencyDecimals, preferredLocale],
);
```

`handleDescriptionInputChange` calls `newDescription.trim()` on every keystroke via `onChangeText`. This silently strips trailing spaces as the user types (making intentional trailing whitespace impossible) and removes leading spaces during composition. The user receives no feedback that their input is being modified.

Trimming should happen on blur or at submission time, not on every change:
```tsx
const handleDescriptionInputChange = useCallback(
    (newDescription: string) => {
        if (!transactionID) {
            return;
        }
        setMoneyRequestDescription(transactionID, newDescription, true, hasReceipt(transaction));
    },
    [transactionID, transaction],
);
```

