If `compute(dependencyValues, context)` throws an exception, `endSpan(spanId)` on line 91 will never execute, leaving an orphan entry in the `activeSpans` Map. Since `recomputeDerivedValue` is called every time an Onyx dependency changes, repeated failures for the same derived key would leak span entries (each `startSpan` cancels the previous one, but if the app recovers and the key stops throwing, the last failed span persists forever). More critically, an uncaught exception here would also prevent the derived value from being written to Onyx, and wrapping in try/finally ensures the span is always cleaned up without changing error propagation behavior.

Wrap the `compute` call and `endSpan` in a `try/finally` block:

```ts
const spanId = `${CONST.TELEMETRY.SPAN_ONYX_DERIVED_COMPUTE}_${key}`;
startSpan(spanId, {
    name: CONST.TELEMETRY.SPAN_ONYX_DERIVED_COMPUTE,
    op: CONST.TELEMETRY.SPAN_ONYX_DERIVED_COMPUTE,
    attributes: {derivedKey: key},
});

try {
    // @ts-expect-error TypeScript can't confirm the shape of dependencyValues matches the compute function's parameters
    const newDerivedValue = compute(dependencyValues, context);
    Log.info(`[OnyxDerived] updating value for ${key} in Onyx`);
    derivedValue = newDerivedValue;
    setDerivedValue(key, derivedValue);
} finally {
    endSpan(spanId);
}
```

